13.3 Flows with Variable Fluid Properties

Fluid properties
Main properties of a fluid:
ρ = density
μ = dynamic viscosity
ν = kinematic viscosity = μ / ρ
k = thermal conductivity
cp = specific heat capacity
In a constant-property model:
ρ, μ, k, cp = constant
In a variable-property model:
ρ = function of temperature and pressure
μ = function of temperature
k = function of temperature
Main relation:
temperature change → fluid-property change → modification of the flow
For air:
temperature ↑ → density ↓
temperature ↓ → density ↑
Coupling between fluid properties and flow
Temperature can modify density:
T → ρ
Density can modify fluid motion:
ρ → velocity field
The velocity field transports temperature:
velocity field → T
Therefore:
T ↔ ρ ↔ fluid motion
The variables depend on each other and have to be updated during the numerical solution.
Typical numerical sequence:
temperature → fluid properties → velocity and pressure → new temperature → new fluid properties → repeat
The process continues until the solution stops changing significantly.
Outer iterations
Variable properties introduce an additional level of iteration.
At each outer iteration:
current fluid properties → solve governing equations → calculate new properties → solve again
Outer iterations can also be required because of nonlinear convection terms and turbulence-model quantities.
Main purpose:
outer iterations → update coupled quantities → reach convergence
Under-relaxation
Large changes between two iterations can make the solution unstable.
Under-relaxation limits the amount by which a variable can change at each iteration.
Small relaxation factor:
smaller change per iteration → more stable calculation → slower convergence
Large relaxation factor:
larger change per iteration → faster convergence → possible instability
Under-relaxation is particularly useful for strongly coupled or numerically stiff problems.
Steady and transient calculations
Steady flow
No physical change with time is considered.
The iterations continue until:
new solution ≈ previous solution
The final objective is convergence to one steady state.
Transient flow
The solution changes with physical time:
time n → time n+1 → time n+2
The solution from the previous time step is used as an initial approximation for the next time step.
Outer iterations can still be performed inside each time step.
Density variation and buoyancy
Density differences become important when gravity is present.
density difference + gravity → buoyancy
Warm air:
temperature ↑ → density ↓ → air tends to rise
Cold air:
temperature ↓ → density ↑ → air tends to sink
Therefore:
temperature → density → buoyancy → vertical motion
This mechanism is important in atmospheric flows.
Boussinesq approximation
Used when density variations are relatively small.
Main assumption:
density ≈ constant
but density variations are still considered in the buoyancy term.
Therefore:
small density variation + gravity → important vertical motion
The approximation avoids treating the complete flow as strongly compressible while still accounting for thermal buoyancy.
Connection with atmospheric CFD:
temperature variation → small density variation → buoyancy → modification of turbulence
13.5 Free-Surface Flows
Free surface
A free surface is an interface separating two fluids.
Typical example:
air
──────── free surface
water
Other examples: ocean waves, open channels, water around ships and liquid inside tanks.
Main difficulty:
interface position changes with time
Therefore CFD must calculate:
fluid motion + movement of the interface
The interface is part of the solution and is not known beforehand.
Conditions at the free surface
Kinematic condition
The interface moves with the fluid.
fluid velocity → interface velocity
The liquid surface cannot move independently from the fluid particles forming it.
Dynamic condition
The forces acting across the interface must be balanced.
Main contributions:
pressure + viscous stresses + surface tension
13.5.1 Interface-Capturing Methods
General concept
The computational mesh remains fixed.
fixed mesh + moving interface
The mesh does not follow the surface.
Instead, an additional variable is used to determine which fluid occupies each computational cell.
Main example:
VOF = Volume of Fluid method
Volume of Fluid method — VOF
VOF describes the distribution of the different fluids using a volume fraction, usually called α.
For an air-water system:
α = 1 → cell completely filled with water
α = 0 → cell completely filled with air
0 < α < 1 → the free surface crosses the cell
Example:
air → α = 0
air → α = 0
interface → α = 0.3
interface → α = 0.8
water → α = 1
water → α = 1
The interface is identified from the transition of α between 0 and 1.
Movement of the interface in VOF
The volume fraction is transported by the fluid velocity.
fluid velocity → transport of α → movement of interface
If water moves into another computational cell, the value of α in that region changes.
VOF therefore does not explicitly move the mesh.
Instead:
fixed mesh + changing α field → moving free surface
Fluid properties in VOF
The local fluid properties depend on the volume fraction.
Example for density:
α = 1 → water density
α = 0 → air density
0 < α < 1 → intermediate value inside an interface cell
Therefore:
α → local density and viscosity → momentum equation
This allows the same computational domain to contain two different fluids.
Interface smearing
A real air-water interface is very sharp.
Ideal representation:
0 | 1
A numerical solution can instead produce:
0 → 0.2 → 0.4 → 0.7 → 0.9 → 1
The interface becomes spread over several cells.
This effect is called numerical diffusion or interface smearing.
Main numerical objective:
transport the interface correctly + keep the interface sharp
VOF methods therefore require numerical schemes that limit artificial diffusion.
Advantages of interface capturing
The mesh remains fixed even when the interface changes shape considerably.
Suitable for:
waves → breaking waves → droplets → merging → splitting
Main advantage:
large interface deformation does not require large mesh deformation
13.5.2 Interface-Tracking Methods
General concept
The position of the interface is represented explicitly.
interface movement → mesh movement
The computational points on the surface follow the interface.
A free surface can, for example, be represented through its height:
surface height = function of position and time
Main advantage:
interface position known very accurately
Main limitation
When the surface changes significantly:
interface deformation → mesh deformation
Large deformation can produce:
poor mesh quality → numerical problems
Particularly difficult for:
breaking waves
droplets
splitting interfaces
merging interfaces
Therefore, interface tracking is effective for relatively smooth surfaces but becomes difficult when the topology changes.
Interface capturing vs interface tracking
Interface capturing
fixed mesh
interface found using another variable
good for strong deformation
example: VOF
Interface tracking
moving/deforming mesh
interface represented directly
very accurate surface location
difficult for strong deformation
13.5.3 Hybrid Methods
Hybrid methods combine interface capturing and interface tracking.
Main objective:
robustness of fixed-mesh methods + accurate interface position
One possibility is to use a fixed computational mesh together with markers that follow the interface.
fluid velocity → marker movement → interface position
The markers improve the description of the interface without requiring the entire computational mesh to move with it.
VOF-PLIC
PLIC = Piecewise Linear Interface Construction.
VOF provides the volume fraction α.
PLIC uses α to reconstruct the actual geometry of the interface inside each interface cell.
In two dimensions:
interface ≈ straight line inside the cell
In three dimensions:
interface ≈ plane inside the cell
Main process:
volume fraction → estimate interface orientation → reconstruct interface
Compared with a basic VOF method:
better interface geometry → reduced smearing → more accurate surface position
Froude number
The Froude number is important in free-surface flows because it compares inertial effects with gravitational effects.
Conceptually:
Froude number = flow velocity / gravity-wave velocity
Three conditions:
Fr < 1 → subcritical flow
Fr = 1 → critical flow
Fr > 1 → supercritical flow
Subcritical
Gravity waves can travel faster than the mean flow.
Information can propagate upstream.
Supercritical
Flow moves faster than the gravity-wave propagation speed.
Information cannot propagate upstream.
13.7 Meteorological and Oceanographic Applications
Characteristic scales
Meteorological and oceanographic flows can cover very large distances.
Typical relation:
horizontal length scale >> vertical length scale
Example:
horizontal scale → hundreds or thousands of kilometres
vertical scale → kilometres or less
Therefore these flows have a very large aspect ratio.
The dominant physical mechanisms depend strongly on the scale of the problem.
Reynolds number
Reynolds number compares inertial and viscous effects.
Atmospheric flows have very large characteristic lengths.
Therefore:
large length scale → very high Reynolds number → turbulence
Atmospheric and oceanographic flows are normally strongly turbulent.
At large scales:
inertial effects dominate
At the smallest turbulent scales:
viscosity becomes important and dissipates energy
Different scales, different physics
Not every physical term has the same importance for every atmospheric problem.
Very large-scale flows:
Earth rotation important
Smaller atmospheric flows:
three-dimensional turbulence important
Thermally stratified flows:
buoyancy important
Near the ground:
surface friction important
Therefore:
physical scale → dominant processes → appropriate numerical model
Coriolis effect
The Earth rotates.
When atmospheric motion is described in a reference frame rotating with the Earth, an apparent Coriolis acceleration appears.
Earth rotation + moving air → Coriolis deflection
Northern Hemisphere:
moving air → deflection toward the right
Southern Hemisphere:
moving air → deflection toward the left
Importance:
large spatial and temporal scales → Coriolis important
small spatial and temporal scales → Coriolis often negligible
Stratification
Stratification means that density changes systematically with height.
Atmosphere:
density variation mainly connected with temperature and pressure
Ocean:
density variation connected with temperature and salinity
Stable vertical density structure:
vertical displacement opposed → vertical mixing reduced
Stratification therefore influences turbulence and vertical transport.
Hydrostatic approximation
Atmospheric pressure changes with height because of gravity.
For large-scale flows with relatively small vertical acceleration:
vertical pressure-gradient force ≈ gravity
This condition is called hydrostatic balance.
Concept:
pressure decreases with height because less fluid is located above the point.
Hydrostatic approximation:
vertical acceleration neglected → pressure and gravity approximately balanced
Useful for many large-scale atmospheric and oceanographic simulations.
Nonhydrostatic models
The hydrostatic approximation becomes less appropriate when vertical motion is important.
Examples:
convection
strong turbulence
cloud dynamics
small-scale atmospheric structures
high-resolution ABL simulations
In these situations:
vertical acceleration cannot be neglected
Therefore:
high-resolution three-dimensional flow → nonhydrostatic model
Atmospheric Boundary Layer — ABL
The Atmospheric Boundary Layer is the lowest region of the atmosphere directly influenced by the Earth's surface.
Main surface effects:
friction
surface roughness
heating
cooling
Near the ground:
wind speed low
Increasing height:
wind speed increases
Therefore:
surface friction → vertical velocity gradient → wind shear
Wind shear generates turbulence.
Main mechanical turbulence mechanism:
surface friction → wind shear → turbulence production
Atmospheric stability
Three main atmospheric stability states:
stable
neutral
unstable
Stability describes the effect of buoyancy on vertical motion and turbulence.
Neutral condition
Buoyancy has little influence.
Main source of turbulence:
wind shear
Therefore:
neutral atmosphere → mechanically generated turbulence
Temperature effects are relatively unimportant for turbulence production.
Unstable condition
Surface warmer than air above.
warm surface → warm nearby air → density decreases → air rises
At the same time, colder and denser air can move downward.
Result:
buoyancy ↑ → vertical mixing ↑ → turbulence ↑
Typical condition:
daytime surface heating
Unstable atmosphere = buoyancy enhances turbulence.
Stable condition
Surface colder than air above.
cold surface → cold dense air near ground
Vertical displacement is opposed.
Result:
vertical mixing ↓ → turbulence ↓
Typical condition:
nighttime surface cooling
Stable atmosphere = buoyancy suppresses turbulence.
Potential temperature
Atmospheric pressure decreases with height.
When an air parcel rises:
pressure decreases → air expands → temperature decreases
This cooling can happen even without heat exchange.
Potential temperature removes this pressure-related effect.
Potential temperature = temperature that the air parcel would have if moved to a reference pressure without heat exchange.
Symbol:
θ = potential temperature
Approximate stability relation:
potential temperature increasing with height → stable
potential temperature approximately constant with height → neutral
potential temperature decreasing with height → unstable
Potential temperature is therefore more useful than ordinary temperature for analysing atmospheric stability.
Turbulence and energy cascade
Turbulence contains structures of different sizes called eddies.
Energy transfer:
large eddies → medium eddies → small eddies → viscous dissipation
This process is the energy cascade.
Large eddies contain much of the energy and depend strongly on the flow geometry.
Small eddies are more universal and are eventually dissipated by viscosity.
RANS
RANS = Reynolds-Averaged Navier-Stokes.
The instantaneous turbulent fluctuations are not resolved directly.
Instead:
flow = mean flow + modelled turbulence effects
Main advantage:
lower computational cost
Main limitation:
detailed instantaneous turbulent structures are not directly calculated
Useful when the objective is mainly the mean flow or when very high spatial resolution is impossible.
LES
LES = Large Eddy Simulation.
Main idea:
large turbulent structures → resolved directly
small turbulent structures → modelled
The unresolved small scales are represented by a subgrid-scale model.
LES is particularly useful for atmospheric boundary-layer simulations because large turbulent eddies strongly influence:
momentum transport
heat transport
vertical mixing
Compared with RANS:
LES → more turbulent detail + higher computational cost
RANS → more modelling + lower computational cost
Connection between scale and turbulence model
High-resolution three-dimensional flow:
LES more suitable
Large domains or calculations focused on mean quantities:
RANS can be more suitable
Therefore:
grid resolution → resolved turbulent scales → turbulence model
Geostrophic wind
At larger heights, surface friction becomes weaker.
For sufficiently large atmospheric scales, the pressure-gradient force and Coriolis force can approximately balance.
pressure-gradient force ≈ Coriolis force
This is geostrophic balance.
The associated wind is called geostrophic wind.
Inside the atmospheric boundary layer, surface friction must also be considered.
Therefore:
pressure gradient + Coriolis + surface friction + turbulence
With thermal effects:
buoyancy
Main ABL relations
Mechanical turbulence:
surface roughness → friction → wind shear → turbulence
Thermal turbulence:
temperature → density → buoyancy → stability → turbulence
Large-scale atmospheric forcing:
Earth rotation → Coriolis effect → wind direction
Therefore, ABL simulations can involve the interaction of:
pressure gradient + friction + turbulence + buoyancy + Coriolis
13.8 Multiphase Flows
Definition
A multiphase flow contains two or more physical phases.
Examples:
gas + liquid
liquid + solid
gas + solid
Typical situations:
air + water → free-surface flow
air + droplets → spray
water + bubbles → bubbly flow
air + solid particles → particle-laden gas
water + solid particles → slurry
Continuous and dispersed phases
Many multiphase flows contain one continuous phase and one dispersed phase.
Continuous phase
Occupies most of the domain and surrounds the other phase.
Example:
air surrounding water droplets
Dispersed phase
Exists as separate particles, bubbles or droplets inside the continuous phase.
Example:
water droplets inside air
Therefore:
continuous carrier fluid + dispersed particles/droplets/bubbles
Eulerian description
The fluid is described at fixed positions in space.
At every computational cell, quantities such as:
velocity
pressure
temperature
phase fraction
are calculated.
This is the standard viewpoint used in most finite-volume CFD calculations.
Concept:
fixed computational cells → calculate how fluid properties change inside them
Lagrangian description
Individual particles, droplets or bubbles are followed through the domain.
For every particle:
position changes with time
velocity changes according to the forces acting on it
Concept:
fluid flow → forces on particle → particle velocity → particle trajectory
Typical forces can include:
drag
gravity
buoyancy
lift
Computational particles
A real multiphase flow can contain an enormous number of particles or droplets.
Tracking every real particle would be too expensive.
Therefore:
one computational particle → represents many real particles
This group is often called a particle parcel or packet.
Main advantage:
much lower computational cost
One-way coupling
Used when the dispersed phase has a very small influence on the carrier fluid.
carrier fluid → particles
but:
particles → negligible effect on carrier fluid
Procedure:
solve fluid flow → calculate particle trajectories
Example:
very small concentration of lightweight particles.
Two-way coupling
Used when the dispersed phase significantly influences the carrier fluid.
fluid ↔ particles
The fluid affects particle motion.
Particles also transfer momentum, mass or energy back to the fluid.
Therefore:
fluid → particles
and
particles → fluid
Additional models may be required for:
particle collisions
particle breakup
agglomeration
particle-wall interaction
Euler-Lagrange approach
Continuous phase:
Eulerian description
Dispersed phase:
Lagrangian description
Therefore:
fluid solved on mesh + individual particle trajectories calculated
Suitable when the dispersed phase can be represented by particles, droplets or bubbles that do not occupy too large a fraction of the domain.
Euler-Euler approach
Both phases are treated as continuous fields.
Phase 1 has its own:
volume fraction
velocity
properties
Phase 2 also has its own:
volume fraction
velocity
properties
For two phases:
volume fraction of phase 1 + volume fraction of phase 2 = 1
The phases interact through exchange terms.
Possible exchanges:
momentum
mass
energy
Therefore:
more phases → more coupled equations → greater numerical complexity
Homogeneous multiphase model
Simpler approximation.
Both phases are assumed to move with the same velocity.
velocity of phase 1 = velocity of phase 2
This is called the no-slip assumption.
The phases can also share pressure and temperature.
Therefore:
multiple phases → treated approximately as one mixture with variable properties
Main advantage:
fewer equations
Main limitation:
cannot represent important relative motion between phases.
Relation between free-surface and multiphase flows
A free-surface flow is a particular multiphase flow.
Example:
air + water
When there is a clear large interface:
VOF / interface tracking → suitable
When the second phase consists of many small bubbles, droplets or particles:
Euler-Lagrange or Euler-Euler → often more suitable
Therefore, the appropriate method depends on the structure of the phases.
Phase change
Multiphase flows can also involve transformation from one phase to another.
Examples:
liquid → vapour = evaporation, boiling or cavitation
vapour → liquid = condensation
solid → liquid = melting
liquid → solid = solidification
Phase change involves transfer of:
mass + momentum + energy
between phases.
Cavitation
Cavitation occurs when the local pressure of a liquid falls below its saturation vapour pressure.
pressure ↓ below saturation pressure → liquid vaporizes → vapour bubbles form
When the pressure increases again:
pressure ↑ → vapour bubbles collapse
Typical applications:
propellers
pumps
turbines
injectors
Cavitation can cause:
noise
vibration
loss of performance
material damage
Vapour volume fraction
Cavitation models can describe the amount of vapour through a vapour volume fraction.
vapour fraction = 0 → only liquid
vapour fraction = 1 → only vapour
values between 0 and 1 → mixture of liquid and vapour
Unlike a simple VOF interface problem, phase change introduces a source term.
Therefore:
transport of vapour + generation/destruction of vapour
If vapour is generated:
source term > 0
If vapour collapses:
source term < 0
Bubble growth and collapse
Bubble dynamics depend strongly on the difference between local liquid pressure and saturation vapour pressure.
local pressure < saturation pressure → bubble grows
local pressure > saturation pressure → bubble collapses
The Rayleigh-Plesset equation is commonly used to describe the dynamics of a spherical vapour bubble.
Main idea:
pressure difference → change of bubble radius → vapour production or collapse
